From 4114e7af66ae64302761e4ac39e4e1c2198ea1d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 04:14:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 204/210] tee: optee: use the reserved shared memory pool on
 Amlogic SoCs

The OP-TEE builds that Amlogic ships in its BL32 blobs advertise both
dynamic and reserved shared memory. They return
OPTEE_SMC_RETURN_EBADADDR for any non-secure buffer in the low part of
DRAM (below about 0x10c00000 on the boards tested). The driver caches
its first message buffer page at probe, and early boot allocations land
in that range, so every standard call then fails with
TEEC_ERROR_COMMUNICATION.

Ignore the dynamic shared memory capability and use the reserved pool.
Clearing the capability also stops advertising TEE_GEN_CAP_REG_MEM, so
libteec allocates from the pool instead of registering user pages that
may land low. Only S4 is matched for now.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/tee/optee/smc_abi.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/tee/optee/smc_abi.c b/drivers/tee/optee/smc_abi.c
index b8a2bdac3208..111b5aab94fe 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/optee/smc_abi.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/optee/smc_abi.c
@@ -1767,6 +1767,15 @@ static int optee_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The Amlogic vendor OP-TEE rejects non-secure buffers in the low part
+	 * of DRAM, so dynamic shared memory fails depending on where pages
+	 * land. Use the reserved pool it also provides.
+	 */
+	if ((sec_caps & OPTEE_SMC_SEC_CAP_HAVE_RESERVED_SHM) &&
+	    of_machine_is_compatible("amlogic,s4"))
+		sec_caps &= ~OPTEE_SMC_SEC_CAP_DYNAMIC_SHM;
+
 	/*
 	 * Try to use dynamic shared memory if possible
 	 */
-- 
